A String object has an arbitrary sequence of bytes, typically representing text or binary data.
A String object may be created using String::new or as literals.

Some characters can be represented as escape sequences in double-quoted strings, character literals, here document literals (non-quoted, double-quoted, and with backticks), double-quoted symbols, double-quoted symbol keys in Hash literals, Regexp literals, and several percent literals (%, %Q, %W, %I, %r, %x).

Key points:
%q
    Single-quoted string
    No interpolation or escape sequences (except \\, \')
    %q(Hello\nWorld) → "Hello\\nWorld"
%Q or %
    Double-quoted string
    Supports interpolation and escape sequences
    name = "Ruby"
    %Q(Hello #{name}) → "Hello Ruby"
%w
    Array of words (strings)
    No interpolation or escapes
    %w(one two three) → ["one", "two", "three"]
%W
    Array of words (strings)
    Allows interpolation and escape sequences
    lang = "Ruby"
    %W(Hello #{lang}) → ["Hello", "Ruby"]
%i
    Array of symbols
    No interpolation
    %i(foo bar) → [:foo, :bar]
%I
    Array of symbols
    Allows interpolation
    item = "bar"
    %I(foo #{item}) → [:foo, :bar]

%r – Regular Expression literal
    Used for regex, helpful when the pattern includes slashes (/) so you can avoid escaping them.
    Supports interpolation.
    %r{https?://[\w.]+}  # → same as /https?:\/\/[\w.]+/
    pattern = "abc"
    %r(#{pattern})       # → /abc/

 %x – Backtick literal (executes shell commands)
    Executes a command in the system shell.
    Returns the output as a string.
    %x(ls)         # → same as `ls`
    %x(echo hello) # → "hello\n"